WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.414
{happy}Hello, and welcome to Shah Spectrum.

00:00:02.414 --> 00:00:05.334
If you're new here, welcome.

00:00:05.334 --> 00:00:08.809
If you're back again, it's lovely to have you with us.

00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:13.564
{happy}The rupee has been holding near ninety-four to the dollar, and reports say

00:00:13.564 --> 00:00:17.698
the Reserve Bank of India, the RBI, has had a hand in that.

00:00:17.698 --> 00:00:23.176
But it may have done it by making promises it will have to keep later.

00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:27.376
So what has the RBI promised, and when do those promises fall due?

00:00:27.376 --> 00:00:28.800
{happy}Hi, I'm Mittal Shah.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.823
Let's walk through it, step by step.

00:00:30.823 --> 00:00:32.610
{fluent}First, what is a forward contract?

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:40.010
It is a deal made today to buy or sell dollars on a fixed date in the future, at a

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.060
price agreed today.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.459
The RBI uses these contracts.

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:47.460
When it sells dollars forward, it can steady the rupee now, without paying out any

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.544
dollars from its reserves today.

00:00:50.042 --> 00:00:51.881
Now, how big is this?

00:00:51.881 --> 00:00:55.687
One research report says that at the end of September, the RBI's net short forward

00:00:55.687 --> 00:01:01.261
position was about two hundred and thirty-nine billion dollars.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:04.472
Net short means it has sold more dollars forward than it has bought.

00:01:04.472 --> 00:01:09.148
We have not seen the RBI's own table for that figure, so treat it as a reported

00:01:09.148 --> 00:01:10.142
estimate.

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:12.806
So when do those contracts fall due?

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:17.778
The same report says about forty billion dollars falls due within a year.

00:01:17.778 --> 00:01:21.506
About fifty-six billion falls due in one to three years.

00:01:21.506 --> 00:01:26.726
And about one hundred and forty-three billion falls due in three to five years.

00:01:26.726 --> 00:01:31.956
The longer ones are mainly linked to foreign-currency deposits that banks took from

00:01:31.956 --> 00:01:33.264
non-resident Indians.

00:01:33.687 --> 00:01:36.517
Next, why does this matter for the rupee?

00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:41.522
Because forward selling is a way to steady the rupee now and settle later.

00:01:41.522 --> 00:01:46.328
In the latest weekly figure we have, for the week to the twenty-third of October,

00:01:46.328 --> 00:01:50.544
the RBI's reserves were about seven hundred and twenty billion dollars.

00:01:50.544 --> 00:01:54.237
That is the headline figure.

00:01:54.237 --> 00:01:59.373
Some analysts argue that, because of these contracts, the dollars truly free to use

00:01:59.373 --> 00:02:00.859
are fewer than that.

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:03.906
That is their reading, not an official number.

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:06.883
Now, a fair point on the other side.

00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:12.062
One analysis says this position is not by itself a loss, and not a direct claim on

00:02:12.062 --> 00:02:12.967
the reserves.

00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:17.334
What matters is when the contracts fall due, and what the RBI does about them then.

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:21.172
So the timing matters more than the size.

00:02:21.587 --> 00:02:23.594
As that shows, the rupee's level reaches you too.

00:02:23.594 --> 00:02:27.239
When the rupee is weaker, imported goods cost more.

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:31.597
That includes fuel, electronics, and foreign travel and fees.

00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:37.211
Reports say that at its weakest, the rupee came close to ninety-seven to the dollar.

00:02:37.211 --> 00:02:40.585
Around the middle of November it was near ninety-four.

00:02:41.009 --> 00:02:43.356
Finally, what do we not know?

00:02:43.356 --> 00:02:46.889
We do not know how the RBI plans to handle the contracts as they fall due.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:48.662
To sum up:

00:02:48.662 --> 00:02:52.861
the RBI has helped steady the rupee by making promises for later.

00:02:52.861 --> 00:02:58.517
About two hundred and thirty-nine billion dollars, by one estimate, is still to be

00:02:58.517 --> 00:03:02.123
delivered, most of it more than a year away.

00:03:02.123 --> 00:03:06.354
The open question is what happens as those contracts fall due.

00:03:06.773 --> 00:03:07.959
Before you go:

00:03:07.959 --> 00:03:11.750
we covered this in episode three, India's emergency fund:

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.067
why forex reserves fell.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:17.230
{happy}Thanks for listening, and see you in the next episode!

